{
  "summary": "The design has over-engineered itself into a complexity trap across six rounds of debate. Three critical issues remain: (1) The dual dirty-flag system (_deps_edited_dirty and _deps_stage_dirty), the 100-entry unaddressed/50-entry addressed event cap with truncated_count metadata, the four-event-type schema, and the poison-flag cancellation mechanism across both planning and implementation tasks collectively add massive implementation surface area for a v1 feature — this is premature optimization and premature edge-case handling that should be deferred until real usage patterns emerge. A simpler design would use a single dirty flag, a single event type ('dep_status_changed'), a flat 20-entry event cap with oldest-first eviction, and no in-flight task cancellation (just let the task complete and re-evaluate after). (2) The override field auto-clear hook inside resolve_dependencies() violates separation of concerns — a pure resolution function should not mutate feature state as a side effect; this will create subtle bugs when resolve_dependencies is called from read-only contexts like broadcast state computation, and the proposal hasn't addressed whether _save() calls inside resolve_dependencies would cause recursive broadcast triggers. (3) The entire plan_invalidation_check mechanism (moving features back to plan-inbox when a dependency is rejected) is architecturally questionable — it assumes that a rejected dependency always invalidates a dependent's plan, but in practice a feature might depend on a capability that could be provided by alternative means, and automatically moving features backward in the pipeline without human judgment is exactly the kind of surprising automated behavior that erodes trust in the system. A warning/flag approach (mark as 'dependency-warning' without moving) would be safer and more aligned with the existing philosophy that manual moves are always permitted."
}
